# Desktop architecture

Permit Control is an installed, offline-first Tauri desktop application, not a web application. Local SQLite is the primary working store. The Permit Control API is backend infrastructure only. It synchronizes Permit Control with the authoritative plant record when connectivity is available.

Successful field data entry must never depend on API availability. No operational workflow may require launching a browser or navigating to the API hostname. Loss of API connectivity must not prevent the desktop application from launching or accessing locally persisted permit information.

- Keep permit work in the Tauri shell: lists, detail, issue, review, authorization, suspension, and closure.
- Write field entries to local SQLite first. Do not block startup, data entry, navigation, or local reads on health checks or other API calls.
- Call the API only to synchronize with the plant record while connected. Do not send operators to Swagger, a hosted page, or the API host to complete work.

```text
Bad: open https://permit-api/swagger to authorize a permit
Bad: suspend a permit only after POST /api/v1/permits succeeds
Bad: app startup waits on GET /api/v1/health before showing permits
Good: suspension is saved in local SQLite while the API is unreachable, then synchronized later
```
